---
# THE MONITORING STACK WATCHING ITSELF.
#
# THE PROBLEM THIS FILE ADDRESSES is that every other rule in this directory assumes Prometheus is
# scraping, Alertmanager is delivering and Grafana is evaluating. When one of those is untrue, the
# fleet's alerts do not become wrong -- they become ABSENT, which is exactly what a healthy fleet
# also looks like. Silence is the ambiguous signal, and this file is the attempt to make it less so.
#
# THE LIMIT OF THAT ATTEMPT, STATED HONESTLY: a single monitoring host cannot fully watch itself.
# If this machine is off, nothing here fires, and nothing outside it notices. The heartbeat below
# is the only rule that turns that into a positive signal -- it moves the question from "have I
# seen an alert?" (unanswerable) to "have I seen today's heartbeat?" (answerable). A second
# monitoring endpoint elsewhere would be the real fix and does not exist.
#
# POST-MIGRATION AUDIT (2026-08-29): EVERY RULE HERE KEPT. `FlyFederationDown` needs a word, because
# it is the one rule in this directory that reads a Fly-shaped series and it was checked rather than
# waved through: its expression is `up{job="fly"} == 0`, and when `fleet.prometheus.scrapeFly` is
# false that job is not configured, so the series is ABSENT and the rule cannot fire. It is
# correct-by-absence rather than dead -- it costs nothing while the Fly scrape is off and starts
# working the moment somebody turns it on. That is exactly the behaviour a capability that is
# switched off should have, and it is why the rule stays rather than being deleted alongside the
# Fly panels that were quarantined.
groups:
  - name: monitoring-self
    rules:
      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THE DEAD-MAN'S HANDLE. Fires permanently, on purpose.
      #
      # Alertmanager's route for it (alertmanager.yaml) delivers it once every 24 hours. What it
      # proves, end to end, is: Prometheus is evaluating rules, Alertmanager is receiving them, the
      # Telegram bot token is still valid, and the bot is still in the channel. NONE of those is
      # proven by any other alert -- a revoked token or a bot removed from the group presents as
      # silence, which is what a good day looks like too.
      #
      # ITS ABSENCE IS THE ALARM, so it costs a message a day and buys the ability to tell the two
      # kinds of quiet apart. Do not "fix" this rule; it is not a bug that it is always firing.
      # -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      - alert: MonitoringHeartbeat
        expr: vector(1)
        labels:
          severity: none
        annotations:
          summary: "Monitoring heartbeat"
          description: >-
            Prometheus, Alertmanager and the Telegram delivery path are all working. If this stops
            arriving daily, assume the alerting path is broken and check it directly -- do not
            assume a quiet channel means a quiet fleet.

      - alert: TargetDown
        expr: up == 0
        # TEN MINUTES. A node_exporter restarted by an activation is down for seconds; a machine
        # rebooting is down for a minute or two. Ten minutes is past both and still names a
        # genuinely unreachable host the same hour.
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "Prometheus cannot scrape {{ $labels.job }} at {{ $labels.instance }}"
          description: >-
            Everything this fleet alerts on is a comparison against a series from this target, so
            while it is down those alerts are not firing -- they are unable to fire. Note the
            `job`: `node` is a machine, `prometheus`/`alertmanager`/`grafana` are this stack's own
            processes on the monitoring host, and `fly` is the federation endpoint (see
            FlyFederationDown below, which is deliberately separate).

      - alert: FlyFederationDown
        expr: up{job="fly"} == 0
        # AN HOUR, because this one is not urgent and it is somebody else's API. It is a warning by
        # design: nothing in this fleet's alerting depends on it.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Prometheus cannot federate from Fly's managed Prometheus"
          description: >-
            THIS IS THE EXPERIMENTAL HALF OF THE FLY PATH and its failure does not mean the Fly
            apps are unwatched. The verified path is Grafana's `fly-prometheus` DATASOURCE, which
            all 20 ported alert rules query and which does not go through this job at all. First
            check whether the endpoint serves federation at all -- if it does not, this job was
            never going to work and both it and this rule should be removed together rather than
            left red. If it does, the usual causes are an expired read token or the auth header
            having been "corrected" from FlyV1 to Bearer, which 401s with "resolving organization".

      - alert: PrometheusConfigReloadFailed
        expr: prometheus_config_last_reload_successful == 0
        for: 5m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "Prometheus is running with a configuration it failed to load"
          description: >-
            The process is serving the LAST GOOD config, so everything looks normal while the
            change that was just deployed is not in effect -- including any new scrape target or
            rule file. Check `journalctl -u prometheus` for the parse error. Note that on this host
            a config change restarts the unit rather than reloading it (restartTriggers in
            roles/prometheus.nix), so this firing means the restarted process rejected what
            activation wrote.

      - alert: PrometheusRuleEvaluationFailing
        expr: rate(prometheus_rule_evaluation_failures_total[10m]) > 0
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Prometheus is failing to evaluate one or more rules"
          description: >-
            A rule that cannot be evaluated cannot fire, and it fails quietly -- the alert it would
            have raised simply never appears. `rule_group` in the labels names the file.

      - alert: AlertmanagerNotificationsFailing
        expr: rate(alertmanager_notifications_failed_total[15m]) > 0
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "Alertmanager is failing to deliver notifications to {{ $labels.integration }}"
          description: >-
            Alerts are being raised and are not arriving. The likely causes are a revoked
            @kinowobot token, the bot having been removed from the chat, or a message Telegram
            rejected -- alertmanager.yaml sets `parse_mode: ""` precisely to rule the last of those
            out, so if it reappears, check whether that was changed. NOTE THE ORDER OF EVENTS: this
            alert has to travel the same path it is reporting as broken, so it may well not arrive
            either. The heartbeat above is what covers that case.

      # GRAFANA'S OWN ALERT EVALUATION IS NOT ALERTED ON HERE, and that is a gap rather than a
      # decision that it does not matter -- Grafana evaluates all 20 of the ported kinowo rules, so
      # a Grafana that is up and failing to evaluate is a real monitoring outage. It is left out
      # because the exact name of Grafana's evaluation-failure series varies by version and nothing
      # here has checked it against the version in nixpkgs, and a rule written against a metric
      # name that does not exist is worse than no rule: it looks like coverage and can never fire.
      # `TargetDown{job="grafana"}` above covers the coarse case (Grafana not answering at all).
      # The fix is to read /metrics on the running instance once and write the rule from what is
      # actually there.
